Q1. Write algorithm/pseudo code to calculate Fibonacci numbers using non-recursive and recursive method.
A. Non-Recursive (Iterative) Method
Algorithm:
Start.
Read the number of terms n.
Initialize a = 0 and b = 1.
Repeat the following steps n times:
Print a.
Calculate c = a + b.
Update a = b and b = c.
Stop.
Pseudocode:
FIBONACCI_ITERATIVE(n)
1. a = 0
2. b = 1
3. FOR i = 0 TO n-1
       PRINT a
       c = a + b
       a = b
       b = c
   END FOR
4. STOP

B. Recursive Method
Algorithm:
Start.
Read the number of terms n.
Define a recursive function Fibonacci(n).
If n <= 1, return n.
Otherwise, return Fibonacci(n-1) + Fibonacci(n-2).
Call the function for each term from 0 to n-1.
Print the Fibonacci series.
Stop.
Pseudocode:
FIBONACCI_RECURSIVE(n)
1. IF n <= 1
       RETURN n
   ELSE
       RETURN FIBONACCI_RECURSIVE(n-1)
            + FIBONACCI_RECURSIVE(n-2)
   END IF
MAIN()
1. READ n
2. FOR i = 0 TO n-1
       PRINT FIBONACCI_RECURSIVE(i)
   END FOR
3. STOP

Q2. Explain time and space complexity of the recursive implementation to calculate Fibonacci numbers.
Time Complexity: In the recursive method, every function call generates two more recursive calls, except for the base conditions. The recurrence relation is: [T(n) = T(n-1)+T(n-2)+O(1)]. Best case: (O(1)), for base cases (n=0) or (n=1). Worst-case time complexity: (O(2^n)).
Space Complexity: Each recursive call occupies memory in the function call stack. The maximum recursion depth is proportional to n. Therefore, space complexity is (O(n)).

Q3. Explain time and space complexity of the non-recursive implementation to calculate Fibonacci numbers.
Time Complexity: The iterative method uses a for loop that executes n times.
Each iteration performs constant-time operations.
Therefore, the time complexity is (O(n)).
Space Complexity: The algorithm uses only three variables: a, b, and c.
It does not require recursive function calls. Therefore, auxiliary space complexity is (O(1)).
